1
00:00:04,600 --> 00:00:15,112
Open almost any Filipino social media feed, and you may find pictures of rubber slippers, glass marbles and old cartoon shows, shared thousands of times.

2
00:00:15,412 --> 00:00:20,633
The captions all say the same thing: Batang 90s, a child of the nineties.

3
00:00:20,933 --> 00:00:27,712
So why does a whole generation keep looking back? To find out, we have to rewind to the nineteen nineties.

4
00:00:28,912 --> 00:00:37,675
Batang 90s means a child of the nineties: Filipinos who grew up in the nineteen nineties, now mostly in their thirties and forties.

5
00:00:37,975 --> 00:00:45,393
Online, they share memes, old photos and inside jokes that only their generation seems to understand.

6
00:00:45,693 --> 00:00:50,339
But to understand the obsession, you have to see what a typical day looked like.

7
00:00:51,539 --> 00:00:58,381
After school, children ran outside barefoot or in rubber slippers, and the whole street became a playground.

8
00:00:58,681 --> 00:01:05,737
They played patintero across lines drawn on the road, and tumbang preso, knocking down a can with a slipper.

9
00:01:06,037 --> 00:01:13,221
Girls and boys hopped through piko, kicked a sipa high into the air, and leapt over hands in luksong tinik.

10
00:01:13,521 --> 00:01:19,361
When the street lights came on, mothers called them home, and nobody wanted to be the first to leave.

11
00:01:20,561 --> 00:01:29,281
Their pockets were full of treasures: teks cards with comic pictures, glass marbles called jolens, and a wooden spinning top, the trumpo.

12
00:01:29,581 --> 00:01:35,528
Some carried a slingshot, the tirador, and many wore a band-aid from the last fall off a bike.

13
00:01:35,828 --> 00:01:47,023
In 1996, Japan launched the Tamagotchi, a tiny digital pet that beeped for food, and later, kids raced little motorized cars on plastic tracks.

14
00:01:48,223 --> 00:01:56,495
In the early nineteen nineties, the country faced a serious power crisis, with brownouts that could last eight to ten hours a day.

15
00:01:56,795 --> 00:02:05,152
In 1993, Congress passed the Electric Power Crisis Act to speed up new power plants, and the long brownouts eased.

16
00:02:05,452 --> 00:02:12,124
For many kids, those dark nights meant candles, hand shadows on the wall, and ghost stories with cousins.

17
00:02:13,324 --> 00:02:17,308
When the power was on, families gathered around one television.

18
00:02:17,608 --> 00:02:26,776
Educational shows made learning fun: Sineskwela began in 1994, and Hiraya Manawari followed in 1995.

19
00:02:27,076 --> 00:02:34,729
Afternoons also meant cartoons dubbed into Tagalog, and everyone talked about the same episode the next day at school.

20
00:02:35,929 --> 00:02:42,025
By the end of the decade, mobile phones that could send text messages had reached the country.

21
00:02:42,325 --> 00:02:50,320
Texting was cheap and quick, and by 2001, Filipinos were sending tens of millions of messages every day.

22
00:02:50,620 --> 00:02:56,033
Newspapers began calling the Philippines the text messaging capital of the world.

23
00:02:57,233 --> 00:03:03,180
So why do grown-ups miss all this so much? Scientists who study nostalgia have some answers.

24
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:14,525
In 2006, psychologists Tim Wildschut, Constantine Sedikides and their colleagues found that people often feel nostalgic when they are lonely or down.

25
00:03:14,825 --> 00:03:20,815
Remembering those days made people feel closer to others, better about themselves, and happier.

26
00:03:21,115 --> 00:03:32,075
Memory researchers also found that people remember their teens and early twenties more than any other time of life, so the nineties shine brightly for this generation.

27
00:03:33,275 --> 00:03:39,563
Batang 90s grew up just before smartphones and social media filled every hour of the day.

28
00:03:39,863 --> 00:03:49,159
So when they share a picture of a slipper or a marble, they are really sharing a feeling: long afternoons, friends outside, and a simpler pace.

29
00:03:49,459 --> 00:03:53,741
So it is not really about the toys. It is about who they played with.

30
00:03:54,941 --> 00:04:00,611
On a quiet street today, someone sets an old can in the middle of the road and picks up a slipper.

31
00:04:00,911 --> 00:04:05,641
The can goes flying, and children from the neighborhood come running to join in.

32
00:04:05,941 --> 00:04:10,309
And just like that, on one small street, the nineties come back to play.
